  "salt": "K6a3gWTudq7GLQaLfr9-Hg==",
  "text": "Ceremony is a Celtic ritual topic. No rite was shared across the whole Celtic world. The year's great holidays, tied to its turning points, may have belonged to Ireland alone. This means the Beltane fires were not necessarily lit through the whole Celtic world. Rush crosses were not woven everywhere at Imbolc. No shared rites of passage linked the Celtic lands. There was no single rule for marriage or for initiation. Death practices differed as well: some peoples cremated their dead, others exhumed them. The kingly inauguration ritual was practiced throughout Ireland. In that rite a woman stood in for the earth goddess and offered the new king a cup. No other Celtic region of Europe used this rite.",
